{"name":"Bell Blue","hex":"#618b97","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bell","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A percussive instrument made of metal or other hard material, typically but not always in the shape of an inverted cup with a flared rim, which resonates when struck.","An instrument that emits a ringing sound, situated on a bicycle's handlebar and used by the cyclist to warn of their presence."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To attach a bell to.","To shape so that it flares out like a bell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bell"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bell","blocks":[{"text":"A bell is a directly struck idiophone percussion instrument. Most bells have the shape of a hollow cup that—when struck—vibrates in a single strong strike tone, with its sides forming an efficient resonator. The strike may be made by an internal \"clapper\" or \"uvula\", an external hammer, or—in small bells—by a small loose sphere enclosed within the body of the bell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Parts_of_a_Bell.svg/330px-Parts_of_a_Bell.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell","wikidata":"Q101401"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg/500px-13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg/1280px-13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg/500px-13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"13th c. BC bell, Shang dynasty","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A13th-12th_Cent._BC_Shang_Bronze_Bell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/%E9%8A%85%E9%88%B4_Chinese_Bell.jpg/500px-%E9%8A%85%E9%88%B4_Chinese_Bell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/%E9%8A%85%E9%88%B4_Chinese_Bell.jpg/500px-%E9%8A%85%E9%88%B4_Chinese_Bell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chinese bronze bell, 18th–16th century BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E9%8A%85%E9%88%B